WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.185
Lesson one point two.

00:00:02.185 --> 00:00:04.490
How a transaction actually happens.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.247
By the end, you'll be able to

00:00:06.247 --> 00:00:09.117
follow a transaction from signature to finality,

00:00:09.117 --> 00:00:11.226
and estimate what it actually costs.

00:00:12.526 --> 00:00:15.021
Here's why this lesson earns its place early.

00:00:15.021 --> 00:00:18.439
Every single action in this entire program, every

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:22.431
swap, every deposit, every approval, is a transaction.

00:00:22.431 --> 00:00:25.276
If you don't know what's happening between clicking

00:00:25.276 --> 00:00:27.956
sign and seeing it confirmed, every other lesson

00:00:27.956 --> 00:00:29.871
is asking you to trust a black box.

00:00:31.171 --> 00:00:32.461
Here's the plan.

00:00:32.461 --> 00:00:36.116
First, the full lifecycle, from signature to finality.

00:00:36.116 --> 00:00:38.054
Second, gas, gwei and nonce,

00:00:38.054 --> 00:00:40.426
the mechanics behind every fee.

00:00:40.426 --> 00:00:43.008
Third, a real worked example: the same

00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:45.591
swap, costing four times as much, just

00:00:45.591 --> 00:00:47.811
because the network got busy.

00:00:47.811 --> 00:00:50.116
And finally, your checklist and a quiz.

00:00:51.416 --> 00:00:52.346
The lifecycle.

00:00:53.646 --> 00:00:54.946
Here's the journey.

00:00:54.946 --> 00:00:57.161
You sign the transaction in your wallet,

00:00:57.161 --> 00:01:00.001
which cryptographically proves it's really you.

00:01:00.001 --> 00:01:03.157
Your wallet then broadcasts it, sending that signed

00:01:03.157 --> 00:01:06.130
transaction out to the network's nodes, fast and

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.164
automatic, but worth naming, since it's the exact

00:01:09.164 --> 00:01:11.224
moment it leaves your device.

00:01:11.224 --> 00:01:13.204
It sits in the mempool, a public

00:01:13.204 --> 00:01:15.544
waiting area every node can see, until

00:01:15.544 --> 00:01:17.284
a validator picks it up.

00:01:17.284 --> 00:01:19.302
A validator includes it in a block,

00:01:19.302 --> 00:01:22.124
and the network confirms that block happened.

00:01:22.124 --> 00:01:25.057
And eventually, it reaches finality: the point

00:01:25.057 --> 00:01:28.289
after which it can't realistically be reversed.

00:01:28.289 --> 00:01:30.704
Gas is paid whether it succeeds or fails.

00:01:32.004 --> 00:01:33.759
Notice that middle stage.

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:36.594
While a transaction sits in the mempool, it's

00:01:36.594 --> 00:01:40.414
genuinely public: anyone running specialised software can see

00:01:40.414 --> 00:01:42.994
it coming and act before it confirms.

00:01:42.994 --> 00:01:45.747
That single fact is the entire basis of

00:01:45.747 --> 00:01:49.394
Lesson 2.5, protecting your trades from exactly that.

00:01:50.694 --> 00:01:54.169
And notice finality isn't a fixed universal number.

00:01:54.169 --> 00:01:57.243
It differs chain by chain, and Layer 2s have

00:01:57.243 --> 00:02:00.959
their own rules entirely, the subject of Module 5.

00:02:00.959 --> 00:02:04.499
In practice, 'confirmed' isn't a single instant either.

00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:07.856
Most wallets and exchanges wait for several blocks to

00:02:07.856 --> 00:02:11.151
pass before treating a transaction as safe, more for

00:02:11.151 --> 00:02:13.814
a large amount, fewer for a small one.

00:02:13.814 --> 00:02:16.156
That buffer exists because very early

00:02:16.156 --> 00:02:18.374
blocks can, rarely, get reorganised.

00:02:19.674 --> 00:02:21.199
Gas, gwei and nonce.

00:02:22.499 --> 00:02:25.743
Gas is simply the unit of computation; cost

00:02:25.743 --> 00:02:29.066
equals gas used, multiplied by gas price.

00:02:29.066 --> 00:02:31.319
On Ethereum that price splits into a

00:02:31.319 --> 00:02:33.328
base fee, which is burned, and a

00:02:33.328 --> 00:02:35.881
priority tip, to get included sooner.

00:02:35.881 --> 00:02:38.987
Gas price is quoted in gwei, where one gwei

00:02:38.987 --> 00:02:42.233
equals one billionth of an ETH. Your nonce is

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:46.486
your account's own transaction counter, in strict order.

00:02:46.486 --> 00:02:48.448
The mempool is where a transaction

00:02:48.448 --> 00:02:50.541
waits, publicly, before a block.

00:02:50.541 --> 00:02:52.486
And finality is the point after

00:02:52.486 --> 00:02:54.978
which it can't realistically be reversed.

00:02:56.278 --> 00:02:59.673
One of these deserves special attention: the nonce.

00:02:59.673 --> 00:03:03.523
Transactions from one account must confirm strictly in order.

00:03:03.523 --> 00:03:06.326
So a single stuck transaction at a low nonce

00:03:06.326 --> 00:03:09.690
blocks every transaction you send after it, no matter

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.233
how much gas those later ones offer.

00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:14.218
The fix is a replacement: same

00:03:14.218 --> 00:03:16.138
nonce, higher fee, sent again.

00:03:17.438 --> 00:03:20.883
Here's exactly how that gets unstuck, step by step.

00:03:20.883 --> 00:03:23.416
A low-nonce transaction gets stuck, its

00:03:23.416 --> 00:03:25.933
fee too low for current conditions.

00:03:25.933 --> 00:03:27.947
Every transaction you send after it

00:03:27.947 --> 00:03:30.373
queues up silently behind it, waiting.

00:03:30.373 --> 00:03:32.434
Nothing confirms, no matter how much

00:03:32.434 --> 00:03:34.293
you offer on the newer ones.

00:03:34.293 --> 00:03:37.263
And the fix is a replacement transaction: the

00:03:37.263 --> 00:03:41.072
identical nonce, a meaningfully higher fee, which releases

00:03:41.072 --> 00:03:43.203
the whole queue once it confirms.

00:03:44.503 --> 00:03:46.275
Here's how that actually plays

00:03:46.275 --> 00:03:48.348
out, purely as an illustration.

00:03:48.348 --> 00:03:51.576
Sam sends three transactions in quick succession:

00:03:51.576 --> 00:03:54.393
a swap, an approval, then another swap.

00:03:54.393 --> 00:03:56.210
The middle one, the approval, gets

00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:57.808
stuck with too low a fee.

00:03:57.808 --> 00:04:01.024
The third transaction, even though it's a completely

00:04:01.024 --> 00:04:04.483
different action, simply cannot confirm until that stuck

00:04:04.483 --> 00:04:07.760
approval does, because the network enforces the exact

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:09.763
order Sam's account sent them in.

00:04:11.063 --> 00:04:14.030
Split the fee open, and it's really two pieces.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.636
The base fee is set by the network itself,

00:04:16.636 --> 00:04:19.785
and it's burned, destroyed, not paid to anyone.

00:04:19.785 --> 00:04:21.769
The priority tip is what you add

00:04:21.769 --> 00:04:24.295
on top, paid directly to whoever includes

00:04:24.295 --> 00:04:26.880
your transaction, to get it in sooner.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.329
Raise the tip, and you're not paying more for

00:04:29.329 --> 00:04:32.045
the same service, you're bidding to jump the queue.

00:04:33.345 --> 00:04:35.197
Here's a detail that surprises

00:04:35.197 --> 00:04:37.350
almost everyone the first time.

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:40.800
A transaction that reverts, fails, still costs gas.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.097
The network did the actual computation either way; it

00:04:44.097 --> 00:04:47.145
only refunds the gas it didn't need to spend.

00:04:47.145 --> 00:04:49.980
Success or failure changes the result.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.550
It doesn't change whether you paid.

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:56.035
These five words aren't specific to this one lesson.

00:04:56.035 --> 00:04:58.680
Gas appears in literally every transaction

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:00.825
from here on, in every module.

00:05:00.825 --> 00:05:03.033
Nonce issues show up any time you

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:06.320
send several transactions in quick succession.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.687
And mempool visibility is the entire subject of

00:05:09.687 --> 00:05:13.355
protecting your trades, properly, in Lesson 2.5.

00:05:13.355 --> 00:05:14.795
Learn them once, here, and you

00:05:14.795 --> 00:05:16.050
won't need to relearn them.

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.415
A worked example.

00:05:19.715 --> 00:05:22.000
Here's the maths, worked through in full.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:26.210
Start with a typical swap: about 150,000 gas.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.349
Multiply by the gas price, 20 gwei:

00:05:28.349 --> 00:05:30.726
150,000 × 20 is 3,000,000 gwei. Convert

00:05:30.726 --> 00:05:33.165
to ETH: 3,000,000 gwei is 0.003 ETH.

00:05:33.165 --> 00:05:38.920
And convert to dollars, with ETH at

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:44.995
$3,000: that's $9, for the whole swap.

00:05:46.295 --> 00:05:48.668
Now here's the exact same swap, same

00:05:48.668 --> 00:05:51.683
hundred and fifty thousand gas, during network

00:05:51.683 --> 00:05:54.870
congestion, at eighty gwei instead of twenty.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:56.745
Four times the gas price.

00:05:56.745 --> 00:05:59.651
One hundred and fifty thousand times eighty

00:05:59.651 --> 00:06:02.557
is twelve million gwei, naught point naught

00:06:02.557 --> 00:06:05.168
one two E.T.H., thirty-six dollars.

00:06:05.168 --> 00:06:07.548
Nothing about your transaction changed.

00:06:07.548 --> 00:06:09.173
Only the network's mood did.

00:06:10.473 --> 00:06:12.848
Now put that next to a small reward.

00:06:12.848 --> 00:06:15.839
If you're compounding, say, a dollar fifty of

00:06:15.839 --> 00:06:19.156
accumulated rewards, and it costs nine dollars, or

00:06:19.156 --> 00:06:22.667
thirty-six during congestion, just to claim them, the

00:06:22.667 --> 00:06:25.438
gas costs more than the reward itself.

00:06:25.438 --> 00:06:27.566
That's precisely why position size

00:06:27.566 --> 00:06:29.938
matters once you're operating on-chain.

00:06:31.238 --> 00:06:34.474
Here's that same nine-dollar fee, against two very

00:06:34.474 --> 00:06:38.073
different position sizes, purely as an illustration.

00:06:38.073 --> 00:06:42.045
Against a ten-thousand-dollar position, nine dollars is under

00:06:42.045 --> 00:06:45.163
one tenth of one percent, barely noticeable.

00:06:45.163 --> 00:06:48.362
Against a ten-dollar position, that same nine dollars

00:06:48.362 --> 00:06:50.913
is ninety percent of the whole amount.

00:06:50.913 --> 00:06:52.478
The fee doesn't change.

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.303
Only what it's a percentage of does.

00:06:55.603 --> 00:06:58.840
One more habit worth building here: an explorer,

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:02.209
like Etherscan, shows you the status, fee, sender,

00:07:02.209 --> 00:07:05.513
contract called, and every token transfer for any

00:07:05.513 --> 00:07:08.191
transaction, yours or anyone else's.

00:07:08.191 --> 00:07:11.735
Lesson 7.2 covers reading one properly; for now, know

00:07:11.735 --> 00:07:14.361
that it exists, and that it doesn't lie.

00:07:15.661 --> 00:07:17.726
Here's specifically what an explorer

00:07:17.726 --> 00:07:19.756
shows you, for any transaction.

00:07:19.756 --> 00:07:22.606
Its status: pending, success, or failed.

00:07:22.606 --> 00:07:24.811
The fee actually paid, to the cent.

00:07:24.811 --> 00:07:27.276
The sender, and which contract it called.

00:07:27.276 --> 00:07:29.550
And every single token transfer that

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:31.756
happened inside it, in and out.

00:07:31.756 --> 00:07:33.877
All of it public, all of it checkable,

00:07:33.877 --> 00:07:36.705
whether it's your own transaction or someone else's.

00:07:38.006 --> 00:07:39.186
Checklist and quiz.

00:07:40.486 --> 00:07:42.381
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:07:42.381 --> 00:07:45.741
You check the fee estimate before signing, every time.

00:07:45.741 --> 00:07:46.793
You know how to find

00:07:46.793 --> 00:07:48.696
your transaction on an explorer.

00:07:48.696 --> 00:07:50.824
And you know a stuck transaction can

00:07:50.824 --> 00:07:52.838
be sped up or cancelled, using the

00:07:52.838 --> 00:07:54.506
same nonce with a higher fee.

00:07:55.806 --> 00:07:57.016
Question one.

00:07:57.016 --> 00:07:58.846
Your transaction reverted.

00:07:58.846 --> 00:07:59.661
Did you pay?

00:08:04.081 --> 00:08:05.716
The answer: yes.

00:08:05.716 --> 00:08:07.679
You pay for the gas used up to the

00:08:07.679 --> 00:08:10.876
point of failure; the network still did that computation.

00:08:12.176 --> 00:08:13.421
Question two.

00:08:13.421 --> 00:08:16.427
200,000 gas, at 10 gwei, with

00:08:16.427 --> 00:08:19.131
ETH at $2,500. What does

00:08:19.131 --> 00:08:20.096
it cost?

00:08:24.516 --> 00:08:27.906
[[pause 4]] The answer: 2,000,000 gwei,

00:08:27.906 --> 00:08:29.856
0.002 ETH, which is $5.

00:08:31.155 --> 00:08:32.425
Question three.

00:08:32.425 --> 00:08:35.290
Why are all your newer transactions stuck as pending?

00:08:39.710 --> 00:08:42.755
The answer: an earlier, lower nonce is stuck.

00:08:42.755 --> 00:08:44.558
Replace or speed it up, and

00:08:44.558 --> 00:08:46.940
every transaction behind it confirms.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:49.495
Question four.

00:08:49.495 --> 00:08:52.542
Does an unconfirmed transaction in the mempool stay

00:08:52.542 --> 00:08:54.535
private until it lands in a block?

00:08:58.955 --> 00:09:02.118
The answer: no. The mempool is public; anyone can

00:09:02.118 --> 00:09:05.155
see it waiting, and potentially act on it first.

00:09:06.455 --> 00:09:07.770
Question five. You're

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:10.223
compounding $1.50 of rewards, and gas

00:09:10.223 --> 00:09:12.524
costs $9. Should you claim now?

00:09:12.524 --> 00:09:13.579
[[pause 4]] The

00:09:17.999 --> 00:09:20.154
answer: probably not yet. The

00:09:20.154 --> 00:09:22.952
fee costs more than the reward; wait until

00:09:22.952 --> 00:09:25.489
the position, or the reward, has grown.

00:09:26.789 --> 00:09:29.599
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:09:29.599 --> 00:09:30.554
Sign it.

00:09:30.554 --> 00:09:32.989
Watch it wait, publicly, in the mempool.

00:09:32.989 --> 00:09:34.779
See it included in a block.

00:09:34.779 --> 00:09:36.799
Confirm it's reached finality.

00:09:36.799 --> 00:09:39.279
Check the fee before any of that happens.

00:09:39.279 --> 00:09:41.012
And if it's stuck, replace the

00:09:41.012 --> 00:09:42.654
nonce with a higher fee.

00:09:42.654 --> 00:09:45.536
Do those things, and you'll never again wonder what's

00:09:45.536 --> 00:09:48.739
actually happening between clicking sign and seeing it done.

00:09:50.039 --> 00:09:53.589
This is education, not financial advice, and every

00:09:53.589 --> 00:09:56.791
dollar figure in this lesson, the gas prices,

00:09:56.791 --> 00:10:00.132
the ETH price, is an illustrative example; real

00:10:00.132 --> 00:10:02.729
costs move with both, constantly.

00:10:02.729 --> 00:10:05.411
Nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:10:05.411 --> 00:10:08.468
your seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:10:09.768 --> 00:10:13.850
That's how a transaction actually happens: sign, mempool, block,

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:17.228
finality, and what it really costs along the way.

00:10:17.228 --> 00:10:19.212
Next, Lesson one point three:

00:10:19.212 --> 00:10:21.658
wallets, keys, hardware and multisig.
